  "query_preview": "/Thursday through Monday/...\nDrier air will gradually spread eastward on Thursday, with lower \ndesert rain chances mostly coming to an end. However, lower level \nmoisture will still remain in place likely allowing another round of \nshowers and isolated thunderstorms on Thursday across the eastern \nArizona high terrain. Given the drying aloft on Thursday, the threat \nfor heavy rainfall is expected to be much lower than Wednesday. The \nsynoptic flow is forecast to shift more out of the west northwest \nlate this week into the weekend and this will help to bring a \ncontinuous flow of drier air through the region. By Friday, PoPs are \nat most 10-15% in eastern Gila County to near 0% in the Phoenix \narea. Lower level moisture should also eventually get scoured out \nwith surface dew points dropping into the mid 30s to lower 40s \nacross the western lower deserts to the upper 40s/lower 50s in the \nPhoenix area starting Saturday. \n\nTemperatures will mostly be an afterthought for the next several \ndays with highs likely failing to reach 100 degrees in the Phoenix \narea through Thursday to right around normal for the western \ndeserts. Once the drier air moves through the region later this \nweek, temperatures will begin to warm with lower desert highs \nclimbing up to around 105 degrees by Saturday.",
